{"id": "marcus-aurelius-1-1-grandfather", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "From my grandfather Verus [I learned] good morals and the government of my temper.", "citation": "Meditations 1.1", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 1.1", "themes": ["gratitude", "family", "learning"], "note": "The bracketed words are the translator's."}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-2-1-morning", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Begin the morning by saying to thyself, I shall meet with the busybody, the ungrateful, arrogant, deceitful, envious, unsocial.", "citation": "Meditations 2.1", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 2.1", "themes": ["anger", "friendship", "work"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-2-1-cooperation", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "For we are made for co-operation, like feet, like hands, like eyelids, like the rows of the upper and lower teeth.", "citation": "Meditations 2.1", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 2.1", "themes": ["duty", "friendship"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-2-11-this-moment", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Since it is possible that thou mayest depart from life this very moment, regulate every act and thought accordingly.", "citation": "Meditations 2.11", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 2.11", "themes": ["death", "attention"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-2-14-no-other-life", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "no man loses any other life than this which he now lives, nor lives any other than this which he now loses.", "citation": "Meditations 2.14", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 2.14", "themes": ["death", "time"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-2-17-point", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Of human life the time is a point, and the substance is in a flux", "citation": "Meditations 2.17", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 2.17", "themes": ["impermanence", "time"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-3-retreats", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Men seek retreats for themselves, houses in the country, sea-shores, and mountains", "citation": "Meditations 4.3", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.3", "themes": ["simplicity", "anxiety"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-3-own-soul", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "For nowhere either with more quiet or more freedom from trouble does a man retire than into his own soul", "citation": "Meditations 4.3", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.3", "themes": ["simplicity", "anxiety", "the-self"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-3-transformation", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "The universe is transformation: life is opinion.", "citation": "Meditations 4.3", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.3", "themes": ["change", "impermanence"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-7-opinion", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Take away thy opinion, and then there is taken away the complaint, \"I have been harmed.\"", "citation": "Meditations 4.7", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.7", "themes": ["anxiety", "the-self"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-17-ten-thousand", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Do not act as if thou wert going to live ten thousand years. Death hangs over thee. While thou livest, while it is in thy power, be good.", "citation": "Meditations 4.17", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.17", "themes": ["death", "time"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-18-neighbor", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "How much trouble he avoids who does not look to see what his neighbor says or does or thinks, but only to what he does himself", "citation": "Meditations 4.18", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.18", "themes": ["criticism", "attention", "fame"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-24-few-things", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Occupy thyself with few things, says the philosopher, if thou wouldst be tranquil.", "citation": "Meditations 4.24", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.24", "themes": ["simplicity", "work"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-26-profit", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Thou must turn to profit the present by the aid of reason and justice. Be sober in thy relaxation.", "citation": "Meditations 4.26", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.26", "themes": ["time", "self-discipline"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-4-49-promontory", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Be like the promontory against which the waves continually break, but it stands firm and tames the fury of the water around it.", "citation": "Meditations 4.49", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 4.49", "themes": ["adversity"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-5-1-rise", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "In the morning when thou risest unwillingly, let this thought be present,--I am rising to the work of a human being.", "citation": "Meditations 5.1", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 5.1", "themes": ["work", "habit", "duty"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-5-16-dyed", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Such as are thy habitual thoughts, such also will be the character of thy mind; for the soul is dyed by the thoughts.", "citation": "Meditations 5.16", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 5.16", "themes": ["habit", "attention", "the-self"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-5-20-obstacle", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "and so that which is a hindrance is made a furtherance to an act; and that which is an obstacle on the road helps us on this road.", "citation": "Meditations 5.20", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 5.20", "themes": ["adversity", "work"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-6-6-avenging", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "The best way of avenging thyself is not to become like [the wrong-doer].", "citation": "Meditations 6.6", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 6.6", "themes": ["anger"], "note": "The bracketed words are the translator's."}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-6-21-convince", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "If any man is able to convince me and show me that I do not think or act right, I will gladly change", "citation": "Meditations 6.21", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 6.21", "themes": ["honesty", "criticism", "learning"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-6-30-caesar", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Take care that thou art not made into a Caesar, that thou art not dyed with this dye; for such things happen.", "citation": "Meditations 6.30", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 6.30", "themes": ["leadership", "humility", "fame"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-6-30-simple", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Keep thyself then simple, good, pure, serious, free from affectation", "citation": "Meditations 6.30", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 6.30", "themes": ["simplicity", "honesty"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-6-54-swarm", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "That which is not good for the swarm, neither is it good for the bee.", "citation": "Meditations 6.54", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 6.54", "themes": ["duty", "leadership"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-3-worth", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "every man is worth just so much as the things are worth about which he busies himself.", "citation": "Meditations 7.3", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.3", "themes": ["work", "attention"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-8-future", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Let not future things disturb thee, for thou wilt come to them, if it shall be necessary, having with thee the same reason which now thou usest for present things.", "citation": "Meditations 7.8", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.8", "themes": ["anxiety", "time"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-29-present", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Confine thyself to the present.", "citation": "Meditations 7.29", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.29", "themes": ["attention", "time"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-57-destiny", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Love that only which happens to thee and is spun with the thread of thy destiny.", "citation": "Meditations 7.57", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.57", "themes": ["contentment", "the-way"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-59-within", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Look within. Within is the fountain of good, and it will ever bubble up, if thou wilt ever dig.", "citation": "Meditations 7.59", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.59", "themes": ["the-self"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-61-wrestler", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "The art of life is more like the wrestler's art than the dancer's, in respect of this, that it should stand ready and firm to meet onsets which are sudden and unexpected.", "citation": "Meditations 7.61", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.61", "themes": ["adversity"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-7-67-little", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "very little indeed is necessary for living a happy life.", "citation": "Meditations 7.67", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 7.67", "themes": ["simplicity", "contentment", "money"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-8-36-whole-life", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Do not disturb thyself by thinking of the whole of thy life.", "citation": "Meditations 8.36", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 8.36", "themes": ["anxiety", "time"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-8-47-judgment", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "If thou art pained by any external thing, it is not this thing that disturbs thee, but thy own judgment about it.", "citation": "Meditations 8.47", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 8.47", "themes": ["anxiety", "the-self"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-8-48-ruling", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Remember that the ruling faculty is invincible, when self-collected it is satisfied with itself", "citation": "Meditations 8.48", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 8.48", "themes": ["the-self", "freedom"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-8-50-cucumber", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "A cucumber is bitter--Throw it away.--There are briers in the road--Turn aside from them.--This is enough.", "citation": "Meditations 8.50", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 8.50", "themes": ["criticism", "simplicity"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-8-59-one-another", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Men exist for the sake of one another. Teach them then, or bear with them.", "citation": "Meditations 8.59", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 8.59", "themes": ["friendship", "compassion", "leadership"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-9-5-omission", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "He often acts unjustly who does not do a certain thing; not only he who does a certain thing.", "citation": "Meditations 9.5", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 9.5", "themes": ["duty", "honesty"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-9-35-loss", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "Loss is nothing else than change. But the universal nature delights in change", "citation": "Meditations 9.35", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 9.35", "themes": ["grief", "change", "impermanence"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-10-16-be-such", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "No longer talk at all about the kind of man that a good man ought to be, but be such.", "citation": "Meditations 10.16", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 10.16", "themes": ["honesty", "self-discipline"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-11-18-disposition", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "consider that a good disposition is invincible if it be genuine, and not an affected smile and acting a part.", "citation": "Meditations 11.18", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 11.18", "themes": ["compassion", "anger"]}
{"id": "marcus-aurelius-12-4-own-opinion", "sage": "marcus-aurelius", "text": "I have often wondered how it is that every man loves himself more than all the rest of men, but yet sets less value on his own opinion of himself than on the opinion of others.", "citation": "Meditations 12.4", "work": "Meditations", "translator": "George Long", "year": 1862, "source_file": "sources/marcus-aurelius/long-meditations.txt", "under_heading": "Meditations 12.4", "themes": ["fame", "criticism", "the-self"]}
